  "findings": [
    "The adopted cohort table controls the public overview where they differ. The public overview suggests an open mobility semester and Economic Ethics, whereas the adopted 2026-cohort route requires thirty Cluster A credits across three fields and Philosophy and History of Economic Thought. The dated philosophy catalogue agrees with the adopted title. No older cohort or general TiSEM mobility rule is substituted.",
    "Year one has eleven distinct units, sixty credits. Probability and Statistics spans two blocks and counts six credits once, not twice. Improving Society Lab activities in year one are zero-credit; the single six-credit completed unit counts in year two. Year two has eleven distinct units, sixty credits. GROW and software workshops are extracurricular/zero-credit. Thirty-one selected credit-bearing components total 180.",
    "The neutral first adopted pathway is the non-exchange Cluster A route; study abroad follows as optional constrained replacement. Five six-credit Cluster A courses must include EME, BAOR and QFAS. Four first-listed Autumn choices are Combinatorial Optimization, Computational Aspects in Econometrics, Data Analytics for Non-Life Insurance and Games and Economic Behavior; first Spring choice is Auctions, Bargaining and Networks. The menu explicitly identifies Combinatorial as BAOR, Non-Life as QFAS and Games/Auctions as EME, so all three fields are established without relying on the computational row’s unprinted field.",
    "The additional six-credit elective is not unrestricted. The dated 3BC000-B-6 rules expressly allow an additional Cluster A course, distinct from the required five. First remaining Spring Cluster A choice is Econometrics for Policy Analysis. Four Autumn Cluster courses plus Philosophy of Science and Statistics total thirty; Spring Auctions, Policy Analysis, Philosophy and History of Economic Thought and the twelve-credit thesis total thirty. No double counting or fabricated free block remains. Optional exchange is constrained equivalent philosophy/EOR/elective study, not automatically thirty open credits.",
    "The thesis is independent econometrics/operations-research investigation under limited supervision: problem and research plan, literature search and critical appraisal, own research, English written thesis and two mandatory English presentation/discussion meetings. The actual catalogue and EER 4.5.7(iii) require 144 completed EC including Improving Society Lab. The example completes the earlier 120 and Autumn thirty, hence 150 EC including ISL before Spring thesis. The general 120/138 rule does not replace this specific rule.",
    "All thirty-one selected dated course descriptions/outcomes plus the separate elective-rules entry were read. Combinatorial Optimization requires Linear Optimization and Advanced Linear Algebra, completed earlier; Non-Life Insurance requires Probability and Statistics; computational econometrics builds on first-two-year Python, probability and algebra; Games requires mathematical proofs. Games precedes Spring bargaining/auctions. Policy Analysis follows the formal econometrics sequence. No extra elective or prerequisite credits are inserted.",
    "Subject distinctions are preserved: computational econometrics includes relational SQL plus Python data processing and scientific computation; the policy course centres causal identification and dynamic macroeconometrics; non-life centres portfolio/credit risk, credibility and claims reserving. Philosophy of Science and Statistics explicitly includes economic reasoning biases before scientific/statistical inference. The other philosophy course is history and ethics of economic thinkers. These actual contents govern bounded matches and meaningful gaps."
  ]
